Consider forecasting a VAR(\(p\)) process in a theoretical situation, where the parameters of the process are assumed to be known.

In practice, one must use estimated parameters when computing forecasts.

As discussed in univariate time series modelling, we know from probability theory that the optimal prediction of a random variable \(Y\) given another variable \(X\) is the conditional expectation (conditional mean) \(\mathsf{E}(Y|X)\).

Recall the properties CEV1–CEV4 (see also Appendix A).
Here optimality refers to a prediction that has a minimum mean square error (MSE).

When \(\boldsymbol{Y}\) is a vector, we obtain the same result, when our goal is to minimize the MSE matrix. An illustration follows. Let \(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{Y}} = g(\boldsymbol{X})\) be any prediction for \(\boldsymbol{Y}\) given \(\boldsymbol{X}\) (a function of \(\boldsymbol{X}\)). Then \[\begin{equation*}
    \mathsf{MSE}(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{Y}}) = \mathsf{E}[(\boldsymbol{Y}-\widetilde{\boldsymbol{Y}})(\boldsymbol{Y}-\widetilde{\boldsymbol{Y}})^\prime]
\end{equation*}\] is the mean square error matrix of the forecast \(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{Y}}\). Furthermore, we get \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{a}^\prime\mathsf{MSE}(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{Y}}) \boldsymbol{a} \geq \boldsymbol{a}^\prime\mathsf{MSE}(\mathsf{E}(\boldsymbol{Y}|\boldsymbol{X}))\boldsymbol{a}
\end{equation*}\] for all vectors \(\boldsymbol{a}\) (with comfortable dimension).

In other words, the matrix \(\mathsf{MSE}(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{Y}})-\mathsf{MSE}(\mathsf{E}(\boldsymbol{Y}|\boldsymbol{X}))\) is positive semi-definite, written as \(\mathsf{MSE}(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{Y}})-\mathsf{MSE}(\mathsf{E}(\boldsymbol{Y}|\boldsymbol{X}))\geq 0\).
By choosing \(\boldsymbol{a} =(0,...,0,1,0,...,0)\), we observe that the mean square error of any component of \(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{Y}}\) is at least as large as the one of \(\mathsf{E}(\boldsymbol{Y}|\boldsymbol{X})\).
We have \(\mathsf{MSE}(\widetilde{Y}) = \mathsf{E}(Y-\widetilde{Y})^{2}\), when \(Y\) is a scalar-valued.

 
We apply the above result to a VAR(\(p\)) process. First, we form the optimal prediction of \(\boldsymbol{y}_{t+1}\) given that the whole history \(\boldsymbol{y}_{t},\boldsymbol{y}_{t-1},...\) is known.

Given the VAR(\(p\)) process, we have \[\begin{equation*}
    \mathsf{E}_{t}(\boldsymbol{y}_{t+1}) = \mathsf{E}(\boldsymbol{y}_{t+1}|\boldsymbol{y}_{t-j},j\geq 0) = \mathsf{E}(\boldsymbol{y}_{t+1}|\boldsymbol{y}_{t},\ldots,\boldsymbol{y}_{t-p+1}) \equiv \mathsf{E}(\boldsymbol{y}_{t+1}|\mathcal{F}_t)  \equiv \boldsymbol{y}_{t}(1),
\end{equation*}\] where \(\mathcal{F}_t = \{\boldsymbol{y}_{t},\ldots,\boldsymbol{y}_{t-p+1}\}\) denotes information set containing the predictive information. By the independence of the error, we find that \(\boldsymbol{u}_{t}\) and \(\boldsymbol{y}_{t-j}\) are independent for \(j&gt;0\). Therefore, based on CEV2, we get (also in this vector-valued case) \(\mathsf{E}_{t}(\boldsymbol{u}_{t+j})=\mathsf{E}(\boldsymbol{u}_{t+j})=\boldsymbol{0}\) \((j&gt;0)\), and \[\begin{align*}
    \boldsymbol{y}_{t}(1) & = \boldsymbol{\nu} + \boldsymbol{A}_{1}\mathsf{E}_{t}(\boldsymbol{y}_{t}) + \cdots + \boldsymbol{A}_{p} \mathsf{E}_{t}(\boldsymbol{y}_{t+1-p})+\mathsf{E}_{t}(\boldsymbol{u}_{t+1}) \\
    &= \boldsymbol{\nu} + \boldsymbol{A}_{1} \boldsymbol{y}_{t}+\cdots+ \boldsymbol{A}_{p} \boldsymbol{y}_{t+1-p},
\end{align*}\] where \(\boldsymbol{\nu} = \boldsymbol{A}(1)\boldsymbol{\mu}\). Generally, for the forecast horizons \(h\geq 1\), \[\begin{equation*}
    \mathsf{E}_{t}(\boldsymbol{y}_{t+h}) = \boldsymbol{\nu} + \boldsymbol{A}_{1}\mathsf{E}_{t}(\boldsymbol{y}_{t+h-1})+\cdots + \boldsymbol{A}_{p}\mathsf{E}_{t}(\boldsymbol{y}_{t+h-p})
\end{equation*}\] or \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{y}_{t}(h) = \boldsymbol{\nu} + \boldsymbol{A}_{1} \boldsymbol{y}_{t}(h-1) +\cdots + \boldsymbol{A}_{p} \boldsymbol{y}_{t}(h-p),
\end{equation*}\] where \(\boldsymbol{y}_{t}(h-j) = \mathsf{E}_{t}(\boldsymbol{y}_{t+h-j})\) and \(\boldsymbol{y}_{t}(h-j) = \boldsymbol{y}_{t+h-j}\) when \(h\leq j\).

In other words, \(\boldsymbol{y}_{t}(s) = \mathsf{E}_{t}(\boldsymbol{y}_{t+s})\) for \(s&gt;0\) and \(\boldsymbol{y}_{t}(s) = \boldsymbol{y}_{t+s}\) for \(s\leq 0\).

 
By using the linear VMA(\(\infty\)) representation of the VAR process (cf. the analogous results for the univariate case), we can present the forecast as \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{y}_{t}(h) = \mathsf{E}_{t}(\boldsymbol{y}_{t+h}) = \boldsymbol{\mu} + \sum_{j=0}^\infty \boldsymbol{\Psi}_{j}\mathsf{E}_{t}(\boldsymbol{u}_{t+h-j}) = \boldsymbol{\mu} + \sum_{j=h}^\infty \boldsymbol{\Psi}_{j} \boldsymbol{u}_{t+h-j}
\end{equation*}\] from which we can see that the forecast error can be written as \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{y}_{t+h}-\boldsymbol{y}_{t}(h) = \sum_{j=0}^{h-1} \boldsymbol{\Psi}_{j} \boldsymbol{u}_{t+h-j}\ \ \ (\boldsymbol{\Psi}_{0} = \boldsymbol{I}_{K}).
\end{equation*}\] This expression shows that the expectation of the forecast error is zero so that the forecast is unbiased. We also find that the covariance matrix of the error is \[\begin{equation*}
\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\boldsymbol{\Psi}_{j}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_u \boldsymbol{\Psi}_{j}^\prime.
\end{equation*}\] In the case of a normal errors, \(\boldsymbol{u}_{t}\sim \mathsf{nid}(\boldsymbol{0},\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_u)\), the forecast error is also normally distributed \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{y}_{t+h}-\boldsymbol{y}_{t}(h)\sim \mathsf{N} \left(\boldsymbol{0},\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\boldsymbol{\Psi}_{j}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_u \boldsymbol{\Psi}_{j}^\prime \right).
\end{equation*}\] Using this results we can form confidence intervals for the components of \(\boldsymbol{y}_{t+h}\) that we seek to forecast. Let \(\sigma_{i}^{2}(h)\) denote the \(i\):th diagonal element of \(\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\boldsymbol{\Psi}_{j}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_u \boldsymbol{\Psi}_{j}^{\prime }\). Then (using obvious notation) \[\begin{equation*}
    \frac{y_{i,t+h}-y_{i,t}(h)}{\sigma_{i}(h)}\sim \mathsf{N}(0,1),
\end{equation*}\] and \[\begin{align*}
    0.95 &= \mathsf{P} \left\{ -1.96\leq \frac{y_{i,t+h}-y_{i,t}(h)}{\sigma _{i}(h)}\leq 1.96\right\} \\
    &= \mathsf{P} \left\{ y_{i,t}(h)-1.96\sigma_{i}(h)\leq y_{i,t+h}\leq y_{i,t}(h)+1.96\sigma_{i}(h)\right\}.
\end{align*}\] That is, the 95% confidence interval for the \(i\):th component in \(\boldsymbol{y}_{t+h}\) is \(y_{i,t}(h)\pm 1.96\sigma_{i}(h)\).
Finally, in practice and as mentioned at the beginning of this section, unknown parameters need to be replaced by their estimates in forecast computation so that the above formulae hold only approximately.

Note also that the above confidence intervals were presented for fixed \(i\) and \(h\).
When we consider several confidence intervals simultaneously we face the usual problems of constructing joint confidence intervals.

10.3 Instantaneous (contemporaneous) causality
The definition of Granger causality discussed above strictly concerns predictive causality: whether past values of a variable help predict future values of another. It does not address instantaneous (or contemporaneous) causality—that is, whether knowing the value of \(x_{t+1}\) helps to more accurately forecast \(z_{t+1}\) given the history \(\mathcal{F}_t\).
Formally, there exists instantaneous Granger causality between processes \(x_t\) and \(z_t\) if: \[\begin{equation*}
    \Sigma_z(1|\mathcal{F}_t) \neq \Sigma_z(1|\mathcal{F}_t \cup \{x_{t+1}\}).
\end{equation*}\] Here, the variable set \(\mathcal{F}_t \cup \{x_{t+1}\}\) includes the future realization \(x_{t+1}\) in addition to the history \(\mathcal{F}_t\). In other words, adding the contemporaneous value of \(x\) to the information set improves the forecast of \(z\).
 
Symmetry and interpretation. A crucial property of instantaneous causality is symmetry. It can be shown that if \(x_t\) is instantaneously causal for \(z_t\), then \(z_t\) is also instantaneously causal for \(x_t\).

Consequently, this statistical definition does not determine the “direction” of causality.
The direction must be inferred from other sources, such as economic theory. For instance, if theory suggests causality runs only from \(x\) to \(z\), a significant instantaneous relationship can be interpreted in favor of that causal link.

 
Instantaneous causality in VAR models. In the context of VAR models, instantaneous causality is directly linked to the correlation of the error terms.

Bivariate case (\(K=2\)): In a bivariate VAR, there is no instantaneous causality between \(y_{1t}\) and \(y_{2t}\) if and only if the errors \(u_{1t}\) and \(u_{2t}\) are uncorrelated. If the errors are correlated, knowing the realization of \(y_{1t}\) (and thus \(u_{1t}\)) provides information about \(u_{2t}\), thereby improving the forecast of \(y_{2t}\).
General case (\(K &gt; 2\)): In systems with more than two variables, the absence of instantaneous causality corresponds to the structure of the error covariance matrix \(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_u\). There is no instantaneous causality between two groups of variables if \(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_u\) is block diagonal with respect to those groups.
Example: Consider a VAR process for \(\boldsymbol{y}_t = (y_{1t}, y_{2t}, y_{3t})\) with the following error covariance matrix: \[\begin{equation*}
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_u = \left[
\begin{array}{ccc}
2.0 &  0  &  0 \\
0 & 1.5  & 0.4 \\
0 & 0.4  & 1.0 \\
\end{array} \right].
\end{equation*}\] Here, the covariance between the error of \(y_{1t}\) and the errors of the other variables is zero (the top row and left column off-diagonal elements are 0). However, the errors for \(y_{2t}\) and \(y_{3t}\) are correlated (\(0.4\)). Conclusion: There is no instantaneous causality between \(y_{1t}\) and the group \(\boldsymbol{y}_{2t} = (y_{2t}, y_{3t})\).

Testing: Instantaneous causality can be tested statistically using a Wald-type test for zero restrictions on the corresponding off-diagonal elements of the error covariance matrix \(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_u\).
 
