11.1 Introduction to cointegration
Instead of stationary variables, let us now assume that we have two variables, \(y_{1t}\) and \(y_{2t}\), where either or both are nonstationary—specifically integrated of order one, denoted as \(I(1)\).
Cointegration in the bivariate case. An important special case of linear regression between nonstationary \(I(1)\) variables arises when they share a common stochastic trend. Suppose there exists a linear relationship such that for some parameter \(\delta\), the linear combination \[\begin{equation*}
z_t = y_{1t} - \delta y_{2t} = [1 \quad -\delta] \boldsymbol{y}_t
\end{equation*}\] is stationary, \(I(0)\), even though \(y_{1t}\) and \(y_{2t}\) are individually nonstationary \(I(1)\). In such a case, \(y_{1t}\) and \(y_{2t}\) are said to be cointegrated.
Why not just difference the data? An alternative way to analyze variables that are non-stationary (or integrated of order one) is to use their first differences, \(\Delta x_t\) and \(\Delta y_t\), which are stationary. However, if a cointegration relationship exists, differencing is not an optimal strategy because it discards the long-run level relationship (the “common trend”) between the series.
The problem of overdifferencing. To understand why simply differencing all \(I(1)\) variables can be problematic, consider a two-dimensional example where \(y_{1t} \sim I(1)\) and \(y_{2t} \sim I(0)\).
Assume \((\Delta y_{1t}, y_{2t})\) follows a stable VAR(1) process: \[\begin{equation*}
    \begin{bmatrix}
        1-a_{11}\mathsf{B} & 0 \\
        0 & 1-a_{22}\mathsf{B}
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \Delta y_{1t} \\
        y_{2t}
    \end{bmatrix}
    =
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \nu_1 \\
        \nu_2
    \end{bmatrix}
    +
    \begin{bmatrix}
        u_{1t} \\
        u_{2t}
    \end{bmatrix},
\end{equation*}\] where \(\boldsymbol{u}_{t}\) is white noise. If we base our analysis on the fully differenced series \(\Delta \boldsymbol{y}_t\), the second equation becomes: \[\begin{equation*}
(1-a_{22}\mathsf{B})\Delta y_{2t}=(1-\mathsf{B})u_{2t}.
\end{equation*}\] Here, the error term has an MA(1) component \((1-\mathsf{B})u_{2t}\) with a unit root. This means the model for the differenced series becomes a VARMA(1,1) where the MA part is non-invertible. This misuse of differencing (“overdifferencing”) leads to misspecified models that are difficult to estimate.
General definition of cointegration. The situation described above can be formalized for \(\boldsymbol{y}_t = (y_{1t},\ldots, y_{Kt})\). If \(\boldsymbol{y}_{t} \sim I(1)\), but there is a linear combination \(\boldsymbol{\beta}^{\prime} \boldsymbol{y}_{t}\), which is \(I(0)\). then an \(I(1)\) process \(\{\boldsymbol{y}_{t},\ t=1,2,...\}\) \((K\times 1)\) is said to be cointegrated if there exists a nonzero parameter vector \(\boldsymbol{\beta}\) \((K \times 1)\) such that: \[\begin{equation*}
\boldsymbol{\beta}^{\prime } \boldsymbol{y}_{t}\sim I(0).
\end{equation*}\] The parameter \(\boldsymbol{\beta}\) is called the cointegrating (cointegration) vector (CI-vector).
Bivariate example and intuition. To illustrate, consider the process: \[\begin{equation*}
    \left[
    \begin{array}{c}
        y_{1t} \\
        y_{2t}
    \end{array}
    \right] = \left[
    \begin{array}{c}
        \mu_1 \\
        \mu_2
    \end{array}
    \right] + \left[
    \begin{array}{c}
        b \\
        1
    \end{array}
    \right] \tau_t + \left[
    \begin{array}{c}
        \xi_{1t} \\
        \xi_{2t}
    \end{array}
    \right],
\end{equation*}\] where \(\boldsymbol{\xi}_{t} =(\xi_{1t}, \xi_{2t})\) is stationary, such as \(\boldsymbol{\xi}_{t} =(\xi_{1t}, \xi_{2t}) \thicksim \mathsf{iid}(\boldsymbol{0}, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\xi})\), \(\tau_{t} = \sum_{j=1}^t \eta_j\) is a random walk process (containing the stochastic trend, with a fixed initial value \(\tau_0\)) where \(\eta_t \thicksim \mathsf{iid}(0,\sigma^2)\).
Empirical example and interpretation. Consider the aggregate income (\(Y_t\)) and consumption (\(C_t\)) of an economy. Empirically, both series are typically non-stationary. However, the Permanent Income Hypothesis suggests a long-run equilibrium relationship where a stable fraction of income is consumed, implying \(C_t = a Y_t, \, 0 &lt; a &lt; 1\). In logarithmic terms, this linearizes to: \[\begin{equation*}
\log(C_t) - \log(Y_t) = \log(a)
\end{equation*}\] This theoretical equilibrium provides the foundation for a cointegration model. While the individual series \(\log(C_t)\) and \(\log(Y_t)\) may wander indefinitely (stochastic trends), the linear combination \(u_t = \log(C_t) - \log(Y_t) - \log(a)\) is expected to be stationary. Here, the residual \(u_t\) represents the transient deviation from the long-run equilibrium. If consumption drifts too far from income, economic forces will eventually pull it back.
Non-uniqueness of CI-vectors. In the absence of restrictions, cointegrating (cointegration) vectors are not unique.
While the vectors themselves are not unique, the cointegrating space (the subspace spanned by these vectors) is unique.

12.3 FAVAR Model
We can now formally define the FAVAR model to address the omitted variable bias. Assume the “true” process (such as economy) is driven by both observable variables \(\boldsymbol{y}_t\) (\(K \times 1\)) and unobserved factors \(\boldsymbol{f}_t\) (\(r \times 1\)). The joint dynamics are given by a demeaned(!) VAR: \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{z}_t =
    \begin{pmatrix}
        \boldsymbol{f}_t \\
        \boldsymbol{y}_t
    \end{pmatrix}
     = \sum_{j=1}^{p} \boldsymbol{A}^*_j
    \begin{pmatrix}
        \boldsymbol{f}_{t-j} \\
        \boldsymbol{y}_{t-j}
    \end{pmatrix} + \widetilde{\boldsymbol{u}}_t,  
\end{equation*}\] where the coefficient matrix is partitioned as: \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{A}_j^* =
    \begin{pmatrix}
        \boldsymbol{A}^*_{j, r \times r} & \boldsymbol{A}^*_{j, r \times K} \\
        \boldsymbol{A}^*_{j, K \times r} & \boldsymbol{A}^*_{j, K \times K}
    \end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation*}\] If we were to estimate a standard VAR using only \(\boldsymbol{y}_t\), we would effectively be restricting \(\boldsymbol{A}^*_{j, K \times r} = \boldsymbol{0}\). If the factors indeed drive \(\boldsymbol{y}_t\), this restriction is invalid and leads to the omitted variable bias.
Estimation and inference. The FAVAR approach proceeds in two steps:

Factor extraction: Estimate the factors \(\widehat{\boldsymbol{f}}_t\) from a large dataset \(\boldsymbol{x}_t\) using, for example, the PCA methods described above.
VAR estimation: Treat the estimated factors as observed variables and estimate the augmented VAR:

\[\begin{equation*}
    \begin{pmatrix}
        \widehat{\boldsymbol{f}}_t \\
        \boldsymbol{y}_t
    \end{pmatrix}
     = \sum_{j=1}^{p} \boldsymbol{A}_j
    \begin{pmatrix}
        \widehat{\boldsymbol{f}}_{t-j} \\
        \boldsymbol{y}_{t-j}
    \end{pmatrix} + \boldsymbol{u}_t.
\end{equation*}\]
 
Notes on inference: Because \(\widehat{\boldsymbol{f}}_t\) are estimated regressors (“generated regressors”) rather than true observations, standard asymptotic standard errors for impulse responses are incorrect. In practice, bootstrap methods are used to compute valid confidence intervals for FAVAR analysis.

Matrix Norm
For a matrix \(\boldsymbol{A}=[a_{ij}]\) of dimension \(n\times m\) the Euclidean norm is \[\begin{equation*}
    \left\Vert \boldsymbol{A}\right\Vert=\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{m}a_{ij}^{2}}
\end{equation*}\] The same norm can be expressed by applying the standard Euclidean norm to the \(nm\times 1\) vector whose elements constitute the elements of \(\boldsymbol{A}\). Define the vectorization operator \(\mathsf{Vec}(\cdot)\) as follows. Partition \(\boldsymbol{A}=[\boldsymbol{a}_{1}\ \cdots \ \boldsymbol{a}_{m}]\) by columns so that \(\boldsymbol{a}_{i}\) \((n\times 1)\) is the \(i\):th column of \(\boldsymbol{A}\) and define the vector (the vectorized \(\boldsymbol{A}\)) as \[\begin{equation*}
    \mathsf{Vec}(\boldsymbol{A})=\left[
    \begin{array}{c}
        \boldsymbol{a}_{1} \\
        \vdots \\
        \boldsymbol{a}_{m}
    \end{array}
    \right] \ \ \ (nm\times 1)
\end{equation*}\] Then it is clear that \(\left\Vert \boldsymbol{A}\right\Vert\) is the norm of \(\mathsf{Vec}(\boldsymbol{A})\), that is, \[\begin{equation*}
    \left\Vert \boldsymbol{A}\right\Vert =\sqrt{\mathsf{vec}(\boldsymbol{A})^{\prime}\mathsf{vec}(\boldsymbol{A})}
\end{equation*}\]
The latter definition and known properties of a vector norm enable us to see that the matrix norm has the same properties as the vector norm

\(\left\Vert \boldsymbol{A}\right\Vert \geq 0\) and \(\left\Vert \boldsymbol{A}\right\Vert = 0\) if and only if \(\boldsymbol{A}=\boldsymbol{0}\)
\(\left\Vert c\boldsymbol{A}\right\Vert =\left\vert c\right\vert \left\Vert \boldsymbol{A}\right\Vert\) for all \(c\in\mathbb{R}\)
\(\left\Vert \boldsymbol{A}+\boldsymbol{B}\right\Vert \leq \left\Vert \boldsymbol{A}\right\Vert+\left\Vert \boldsymbol{B}\right\Vert\) for all \(m\times n\) matrices \(\boldsymbol{B}\) (triangular inequality)
\(\left\Vert \boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{B}\right\Vert \leq \left\Vert \boldsymbol{A}\right\Vert\left\Vert \boldsymbol{B}\right\Vert\) (submultiplicativity)

A variety of other matrix norms are applied that satisfy the above four conditions (sometimes only the first three ones), but these norms are not needed in this course.
Sometimes it is useful to express the Euclidean norm by applying the trace of a square matrix, i.e., the sum of diagonal elements. If \(\boldsymbol{B}=[b_{ij}]\) is \(n\times n\) matrix, then its trace \(\mathsf{tr}(\boldsymbol{B})\) is defined by \[\begin{equation*}
    \mathsf{tr}(\boldsymbol{B})=\sum_{i=1}^{n}b_{ii}
\end{equation*}\] It is easy to see from the definition that \[\begin{equation*}
    \mathsf{tr}(\boldsymbol{B}+\boldsymbol{C})=\mathsf{tr}(\boldsymbol{B})+\mathsf{tr}(\boldsymbol{C})\text{ and }\mathsf{tr}(\boldsymbol{B}\boldsymbol{D})=\mathsf{tr}(\boldsymbol{D}\boldsymbol{B})
\end{equation*}\] where \(\boldsymbol{C}\) is \(n\times n\) matrix, while in the latter condition \(\boldsymbol{B}\) can be \(n\times m\) and \(\boldsymbol{D}\) can be \(m\times n\).
The norm of the matrix \(\boldsymbol{A}\) (\(n\times m\)) can be written \[\begin{equation*}
    \left\Vert \boldsymbol{A}\right\Vert =\sqrt{\mathsf{tr}(\boldsymbol{A}^{\prime}\boldsymbol{A})}
\end{equation*}\] The matrix norm can be used to analyze the convergence of matrix sequences and series.

Matrix Eigenvalues and Jordan Decomposition
The eigenvalues \(\lambda_{1},...,\lambda _{n}\) of a square matrix \(\boldsymbol{A}\) \((n\times n)\) satisfy (are solutions to) the determinant equation \[\begin{equation*}
    \det (\boldsymbol{A}-\lambda \boldsymbol{I}_{n})=0
\end{equation*}\] The corresponding eigenvectors \(\boldsymbol{u}_{1},...,\boldsymbol{u}_{n}\) (\(\boldsymbol{u}_{i}\neq \boldsymbol{0}\)) are obtained from the equations \[\begin{equation*}
    (\boldsymbol{A}-\lambda_{i}\boldsymbol{I}_{n})\boldsymbol{u}_{i}=\boldsymbol{0}\Longleftrightarrow \boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{u}_{i}=\lambda _{i}\boldsymbol{u}_{i}, \quad i=1,...,n
\end{equation*}\]
If \(\boldsymbol{A}\) \((n\times n)\) and \(\lambda_{1},...,\lambda _{n}\) are as above, there exists a non-singular matrix \(\boldsymbol{P}\) \((n\times n)\) such that \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{A}=\boldsymbol{P}\boldsymbol{\Lambda} \boldsymbol{P}^{-1}
\end{equation*}\] Here \(\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\) \((n\times n)\) is the block diagonal matrix \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{\Lambda} =\left[
    \begin{array}{cccc}
        \boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{n_{1}} & \boldsymbol{0} & \cdots & \boldsymbol{0} \\
        \boldsymbol{0} & \boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{n_{2}} & \cdots & \boldsymbol{0} \\
        \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
        \boldsymbol{0} & \boldsymbol{0} & \cdots & \boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{n_{k}}
    \end{array}
    \right] \equiv \mathsf{diag}[\boldsymbol{\Lambda} _{n_{1}}\ \cdots \ \boldsymbol{\Lambda} _{n_{k}}]
\end{equation*}\] where \(n_{1}+\cdots +n_{k}=n\) and \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{\Lambda} _{n_{i}}=\left[
    \begin{array}{cccccc}
        \lambda_{i} & 1 & 0 & \cdots & \cdots & 0 \\
        0 & \lambda_{i} & 1 &  &  & 0 \\
        \vdots &  & \ddots &  &  & \vdots \\
        \vdots &  &  & \ddots &  & 0 \\
        \vdots &  &  & 0 & \lambda_{i} & 1 \\
        0 & \cdots & \cdots & \cdots & 0 & \lambda_{i}
    \end{array}
    \right]
\end{equation*}\] The cells on the main diagonal of \(\boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{n_{i}}\) contain the eigenvalue \(\lambda_{i}\), the cells of the diagonal right above the main diagonal contain \(1\) and all other cells contain \(0\) (the same eigenvalue may appear in several blocks of \(\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\)). This representation of matrix \(\boldsymbol{A}\) is called the or the . If the eigenvalues of \(\boldsymbol{A}\) are distinct, the matrix \(\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\) is diagonal and its diagonal elements are the eigenvalues of \(\boldsymbol{A}\), while the columns of \(\boldsymbol{P}\) (that are linearly independent) are the eigenvectors of \(\boldsymbol{A}\).
By computing we can show the following useful result (where \(\binom{k}{j}=0\), if \(j&gt;k\)) \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{\Lambda} _{n_{i}}^{k}=\left[
    \begin{array}{cccccc}
        \lambda _{i}^{k} & \binom{k}{1}\lambda _{i}^{k-1} & \cdots & \binom{k}{j}
        \lambda _{i}^{k-j} & \cdots & \binom{k}{n_{i}-1}\lambda _{i}^{k-n_{i}+1} \\
        0 & \ddots & \ddots &  & \ddots &  \\
        \vdots & 0 & \ddots & \ddots &  & \ddots \\
        \vdots &  & 0 & \ddots & \ddots &  \\
        \vdots &  &  & 0 & \ddots & \ddots \\
        0 & \cdots & \cdots & \cdots & 0 & \ddots
    \end{array}
    \right] ,
\end{equation*}\] where each diagonal carries the same element (and all elements below the main diagonal are zeros).

Matrix Sequences and Series
The convergence of a matrix sequence \(\boldsymbol{A}_{N}=[a_{ij,N}]\), \(N=1,2,...\) to the matrix \(\boldsymbol{A}=[a_{ij}]\) (of the same dimension) may be defined to require that \(a_{ij,N}\rightarrow a_{ij}\) for all \(i,j\), as \(N\rightarrow \infty\). This is denoted by \(\lim_{N\rightarrow \infty }\boldsymbol{A}_{N}=\boldsymbol{A}\) or \(\boldsymbol{A}_{N}\rightarrow \boldsymbol{A}\) (as \(N\rightarrow \infty\)).
The convergence of a matrix series \(\boldsymbol{S}=\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\boldsymbol{A}_{i}\) is defined as the limit of the sequence of partial sums \(\boldsymbol{S}_{N}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\boldsymbol{A}_{i}\), that is, there exists a matrix \(\boldsymbol{S}\) such that \(\boldsymbol{S}_{N}\rightarrow \boldsymbol{S}\), which implies that \(\boldsymbol{S}=\sum_{i=1}^{\infty }\boldsymbol{A}_{i}\).
If \(\boldsymbol{A}=[a_{ij}]\) is \(n\times m\) matrix, then the definition of the matrix norm implies \(\max \left\{\left\vert a_{ij}\right\vert \right\} \leq \left\Vert \boldsymbol{A}\right\Vert \leq \sqrt{nm}\max \left\{ \left\vert a_{ij}\right\vert \right\}\), where maximization is over \(i=1,...,n\) and \(j=1,...,m\). Using this together with the definition of convergence of real sequences we observe that for the matrix sequence \(\boldsymbol{A}_{N}\) it holds that \(\boldsymbol{A}_{N}\rightarrow \boldsymbol{A}\) if and only if \(\left\Vert \boldsymbol{A}_{N}-\boldsymbol{A}\right\Vert \rightarrow 0\). An analogous alternative definition can also be presented for matrix series. Given the above notes and well known results on the convergence of real valued series it is clear that a sufficient condition for a matrix series to converge is that the real valued series \(\sum_{i=1}^{\infty }\left\Vert \boldsymbol{A}_{i}\right\Vert\) converges (absolute convergence).
Let \(\boldsymbol{A}\) be an \(n\times n\) square matrix and \(\lambda _{1},...,\lambda _{n}\) its eigenvalues. Assume \(\rho \equiv \max_{1\leq i\leq n}\left\vert \lambda_{i}\right\vert &lt;1\). By applying the Jordan decomposition, we obtain \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{A}^{N}=(\boldsymbol{P}\boldsymbol{\Lambda} \boldsymbol{P}^{-1})^{N}=\boldsymbol{P}\boldsymbol{\Lambda} ^{N}\boldsymbol{P}^{-1}
\end{equation*}\] Because \(N^{k}\rho^{N}\rightarrow 0\), as \(N\rightarrow\infty\), for all \(k\geq 0\), we obtain using results from Section A.2 that \(\boldsymbol{\Lambda}^{N}\rightarrow \boldsymbol{0}\) and hence, given the above equation, we have \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{A}^{N}\rightarrow \boldsymbol{0}\text{,\ as }N\rightarrow \infty
\end{equation*}\] Choose \(0&lt;\overline{\lambda }&lt;1\) such that \(\rho &lt;\overline{\lambda }&lt;1\) (we can always find such \(\overline{\lambda }\)). Then, \(N^{k}\rho^{N}=N^{k}\left(\rho /\overline{\lambda }\right)^{N}\overline{\lambda }^{N}\), where \(N^{k}\left( \rho /\overline{\lambda }\right)^{N}\rightarrow 0\). Hence \(N^{k}\rho^{N}&lt;\overline{\lambda}^{N}\) for all sufficiently large \(N\). It follows that the convergence \(\boldsymbol{\Lambda}^{N}\rightarrow \boldsymbol{0}\) is geometric, that is, there exists a constant \(C&lt;\infty\) and a constant \(r&lt;1\) such that for the matrix elements \([\boldsymbol{\Lambda} ^{N}]_{ij}\) it holds that \([\boldsymbol{\Lambda} ^{N}]_{ij}\leq Cr^{N}\). The same applies to the elements of the matrix \(\boldsymbol{A}^{N}=\boldsymbol{P}\boldsymbol{\Lambda} ^{N}\boldsymbol{P}^{-1}\). By the submultiplicativity of a matrix it also follows that \[\begin{equation*}
    \left\Vert \boldsymbol{A}^{N}\right\Vert =\left\Vert \boldsymbol{P}\boldsymbol{\Lambda} ^{N}\boldsymbol{P}^{-1}\right\Vert \leq\left\Vert \boldsymbol{P}\right\Vert \left\Vert \boldsymbol{P}^{-1}\right\Vert \left\Vert \boldsymbol{\Lambda}^{N}\right\Vert
\end{equation*}\] so that \(\left\Vert \boldsymbol{A}^{N}\right\Vert \rightarrow 0\) geometrically (If the eigenvalues of \(\boldsymbol{A}\) are distinct, \(\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\) is diagonal with diagonal elements among the eigenvalues of \(\boldsymbol{A}\) that are smaller than one in absolute value. Hence the argument behind the result becomes simpler). Altogether we can conclude that \(\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\boldsymbol{A}_{i}\) converges (\(\boldsymbol{A}^{0}=\boldsymbol{I}_{n}\)) and it is easily seen that \[\begin{equation*}
    \sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\boldsymbol{A}_{i}=\left(\boldsymbol{I}_{n}-\boldsymbol{A}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation*}\]

Polynomials, Polynomial Matrices and Power Series
Consider the \(m\):th degree (or order) polynomial function \[\begin{equation*}
    p(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{m}a_{k}z^{k}
\end{equation*}\] where \(a_{k}\) and \(z\) are real or complex numbers and \(a_{m}\neq 0\). When \(z\in \mathbb{C}\), the fundamental theorem of algebra implies that we can write \[\begin{equation*}
    p(z)=a_{m}\left( z-\zeta _{1}\right) \cdots \left( z-\zeta _{m}\right) ,
\end{equation*}\] where \(\zeta _{1},...,\zeta _{m}\) are the roots of \(p(z)\) such that \(p(\zeta_{k})=0\). The case \(\zeta _{k}=\zeta _{l}\) (\(k\neq l\)) is possible and when the coefficients \(a_{k}\) are real (\(a_{k}\in \mathbb{R}\) for all \(k\)) possible complex roots appear as conjugate pairs so that if \(\zeta_{k}=x_{k}+iy_{k}\) (\(i^{2}=-1\)) then for some \(l\neq k\), \(\zeta_{l}=x_{l}-iy_{l}\equiv \overline{\zeta}_{l}\).
A is a matrix whose elements are polynomials \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{P}(z)=[p_{ij}(z)],\ \ p_{ij}(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{m_{ij}}a_{ij,k}z^{k}
\end{equation*}\] Setting \(m=\max \left\{ m_{ij}\right\}\) we can write \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{P}(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{m}\boldsymbol{A}_{k}z^{k},
\end{equation*}\] where \(\boldsymbol{A}_{k}=[a_{ij,k}]\) and \(a_{ij,k}=0\), when \(k&gt;m_{ij}\). Given this presentation it is customary to talk about matrix polynomials, that is, polynomials whose coefficients are matrices.
Calculations such as \(\boldsymbol{A}(z)+\boldsymbol{B}(z)\), \(\boldsymbol{A}(z)\boldsymbol{B}(z)\) and the determinant \(\det(\boldsymbol{A}(z))\) are defined as in the case of usual matrices. For a square matrix polynomial \(\boldsymbol{A}(z)\) \((n\times n)\) the inverse matrix \(\boldsymbol{A}(z)^{-1}\) \((n\times n)\) satisfies \(\boldsymbol{A}(z)\boldsymbol{A}(z)^{-1}=\boldsymbol{I}_{n}\). As in the case of matrix whose elements are real numbers, we can compute the inverse matrix \(\boldsymbol{A}(z)^{-1}\) by the formula \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{A}(z)^{-1}=\frac{1}{\det (\boldsymbol{A}(z))}\boldsymbol{A}(z)^{\ast }\ \ (\det (\boldsymbol{A})\neq 0)
\end{equation*}\] where \(\boldsymbol{A}(z)^{\ast }=[a_{ij}^{\ast}(z)]\) is the adjunct matrix of \(\boldsymbol{A}(z)\) such that \(a_{ij}^{\ast }(z)=(-1)^{i+j}\times \det (\boldsymbol{M}_{ij})\), where \(\boldsymbol{M}_{ij}\) is the \((n-1)\times (n-1)\) matrix that results from deleting row \(i\) and column \(j\) from \(\boldsymbol{A}\) (the same definition applies to the usual matrix). Given that a determinant can be formed by summing products of elements of the matrix, both \(\det(\boldsymbol{A}(z))\) and \(\boldsymbol{A}(z)^{\ast}\) are polynomials and the degree of \(\det (\boldsymbol{A}(z))\) is at most \(mn\). Hence, the elements of the inverse matrix \(\boldsymbol{A}(z)^{-1}\) are rational functions of the form \(\sum_{j=0}^{q}a_{j}z^{j}/\sum_{j=0}^{r}b_{j}z^{j}\ (q,r&lt;\infty)\).
Return to the usual polynomial \(p(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{m}a_{k}z^{k}\). It is easy to see that we can write \[\begin{equation}
    p(z)=p(1)+(1-z)q(z)  
    \label{eq:A1}
\end{equation}\] where \[\begin{equation*}
    q(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{m-1}b_{k}z^{k},\ \ b_{k}=-\sum_{j=k+1}^{m}a_{j}, \ k=1,...,m-1
\end{equation*}\] Another similar representation is \[\begin{equation}
    p(z)=p(1)z+(1-z)r(z)
    \label{eq:A2}
\end{equation}\] where \[\begin{equation*}
    r(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{m-1}c_{k}z^{k},\ c_{0}=a_{0},\ c_{k}=-\sum_{j=k+1}^{m}a_{j},\ k=1,...,m-1
\end{equation*}\] These representations can be generalized to the case of power series \[\begin{equation*}
    p(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty }a_{k}z^{k}\ \ \ (\left\vert z\right\vert \leq 1)
\end{equation*}\] If we assume \[\begin{equation*}
    \sum_{k=0}^{\infty }k\left\vert a_{k}\right\vert &lt;\infty
\end{equation*}\] it can be easily proved that equations hold when \(\left\vert z\right\vert \leq 1\) and \(m\) is replaced by \(\infty\). Furthermore, it holds that \(\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left\vert b_{k}\right\vert &lt;\infty\) and \(\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left\vert c_{k}\right\vert &lt;\infty\).
The above stated results generalize to matrix polynomials. Let \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{P}(z)=[p_{ij}(z)]=\sum_{k=0}^{m}\boldsymbol{A}_{k}z^{k}\ \ \ (\left\vert z\right\vert \leq 1)
\end{equation*}\] where \(\boldsymbol{A}_{k}=[a_{ij,k}]\) satisfies the condition \[\begin{equation*}
    \sum_{k=0}^{\infty }k\left\Vert \boldsymbol{A}_{k}\right\Vert &lt;\infty
\end{equation*}\] By applying the above derivations to the polynomials \(p_{ij}(z)\) we can show the following generalization \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{P}(z)=\boldsymbol{P}(1)z+(1-z)\boldsymbol{Q}(z)  
\end{equation*}\] where \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{Q}(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty }\boldsymbol{B}_{k}z^{k},\ \boldsymbol{B}_{k}=-\sum_{j=k+1}^{\infty }\boldsymbol{A}_{j} \text{ and }\sum_{k=1}^{\infty }\left\Vert \boldsymbol{B}_{k}\right\Vert &lt;\infty
\end{equation*}\] Similarly we can generalize \(\eqref{eq:A2}\) to \[\begin{equation}
    \boldsymbol{P}(z)=\boldsymbol{P}(1)z+(1-z)\boldsymbol{R}(z)
\end{equation}\] where \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{R}(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty }\boldsymbol{C}_{k}z^{k},\ \boldsymbol{C}_{0}=\boldsymbol{A}_{0},\ \boldsymbol{C}_{k}=-\sum_{j=k+1}^{\infty }\boldsymbol{A}_{j},\ k\geq 1\ \text{and }\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\left\Vert \boldsymbol{C}_{k}\right\Vert &lt;\infty
\end{equation*}\] If \(\boldsymbol{P}(z)\) is degree \(m\) matrix polynomial (i.e., \(\boldsymbol{A}_{k}=\boldsymbol{0}\) for all \(k&gt;m\)), then \(\boldsymbol{Q}(z)\) and \(\boldsymbol{R}(z)\) are degree \(m-1\) matrix polynomials.

Partitioned Matrices and the Kronecker Product
A partitioned matrix is a matrix whose elements are matrices \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{A}=[\boldsymbol{A}_{ij}]=\left[
    \begin{array}{ccc}
        \boldsymbol{A}_{11} & \cdots & \boldsymbol{A}_{1m} \\
        \vdots &  & \vdots \\
        \boldsymbol{A}_{n1} & \cdots & \boldsymbol{A}_{nm}
    \end{array}
    \right]
\end{equation*}\] If \(\boldsymbol{A}=[\boldsymbol{A}_{ij}]\) and \(\boldsymbol{B}=[\boldsymbol{B}_{ij}]\), then (assuming that the dimensions agree with the definitions of the calculations) \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{A}+\boldsymbol{B}=[\boldsymbol{A}_{ij}+\boldsymbol{B}_{ij}]\text{ and }\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{B}=\left[\sum_{k}\boldsymbol{A}_{ik}\boldsymbol{B}_{kj}\right]
\end{equation*}\] These results follow from standard matrix addition and multiplication operations. In addition, when \(\det (\boldsymbol{A})\neq 0\) and \(\det (\boldsymbol{A}_{ii})\neq 0\
(i=1,2)\) we have \[\begin{equation*}
    \left[
    \begin{array}{cc}
        \boldsymbol{A}_{11} & \boldsymbol{A}_{12} \\
        \boldsymbol{A}_{21} & \boldsymbol{A}_{22}
    \end{array}%
    \right] ^{-1}=\left[
    \begin{array}{cc}
        \boldsymbol{A}_{1\cdot 2}^{-1} & -\boldsymbol{A}_{11}^{-1}\boldsymbol{A}_{12}\boldsymbol{A}_{2\cdot 1}^{-1} \\
        -\boldsymbol{A}_{22}^{-1}\boldsymbol{A}_{21}\boldsymbol{A}_{1\cdot 2}^{-1} & \boldsymbol{A}_{2\cdot 1}^{-1}
    \end{array}
    \right] ,
\end{equation*}\] where \(\boldsymbol{A}_{i\cdot j}=\boldsymbol{A}_{ii}-\boldsymbol{A}_{ij}\boldsymbol{A}_{jj}^{-1}\boldsymbol{A}_{ji}\). It also holds that (a) \(\det (\boldsymbol{A})=\det (\boldsymbol{A}_{11})\det (\boldsymbol{A}_{22})\), when \(\boldsymbol{A}_{12}=\boldsymbol{0}\) or \(\boldsymbol{A}_{21}=\boldsymbol{0}\), (b) \(\det (\boldsymbol{A})=\det (\boldsymbol{A}_{11})\det (\boldsymbol{A}_{2\cdot 1})\), when \(\det (\boldsymbol{A}_{11})\neq 0\), and (c) \(\det (\boldsymbol{A})=\det (\boldsymbol{A}_{22})\det (\boldsymbol{A}_{1\cdot 2})\), when \(\det (\boldsymbol{A}_{22})\neq 0\).
The Kronecker product of \(\boldsymbol{A}\) \((n\times m)\) and \(\boldsymbol{B}\) \((p\times q)\) is the \(np\times mq\) matrix \[\begin{equation*}
    \boldsymbol{A}\otimes \boldsymbol{B}=\left[
    \begin{array}{ccc}
        a_{11}\boldsymbol{B} & \cdots & a_{1m}\boldsymbol{B} \\
        \vdots &  & \vdots \\
        a_{n1}\boldsymbol{B} & \cdots & a_{nm}\boldsymbol{B}
    \end{array}
    \right] =[a_{ij}\boldsymbol{B}]
\end{equation*}\] The Kronecker product has the following properties

Differentiation Rules
When \(f:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow \mathbb{R}\) is differentiable, we denote \[\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\partial}{\partial \boldsymbol{x}}f(\boldsymbol{x})=\left[\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{1}}f(\boldsymbol{x})\ \cdots \ \frac{\partial }{\partial x_{n}}f(\boldsymbol{x})\right] ^{\prime }\ \ (n\times 1)
\end{equation*}\] and \[\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\partial }{\partial \boldsymbol{x}^{\prime }}f(\boldsymbol{x})=\left[ \frac{\partial }{\partial x_{1}}f(\boldsymbol{x})\ \cdots \ \frac{\partial }{\partial x_{n}}f(\boldsymbol{x})\right] ^{\prime }\ \ (1\times n)
\end{equation*}\] When \(\boldsymbol{f}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{m}\) is differentiable, we denote \[\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\partial }{\partial \boldsymbol{x}}\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x})=\left[
    \begin{array}{ccc}
        \frac{\partial }{\partial x_{1}}f_{1}(\boldsymbol{x}) & \cdots & \frac{\partial }{\partial x_{1}}f_{m}(\boldsymbol{x}) \\
        \vdots &  & \vdots \\
        \frac{\partial }{\partial x_{n}}f_{m}(\boldsymbol{x}) & \cdots & \frac{\partial }{\partial x_{n}}f_{m}(\boldsymbol{x})
    \end{array}
    \right]
\end{equation*}\]
It is easy to check that \(\partial \boldsymbol{a}^{\prime }\boldsymbol{x}/\partial \boldsymbol{x}=\boldsymbol{a}\ (n\times 1)\) and \(\partial \boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{x}/\partial \boldsymbol{x}=\boldsymbol{A}^{\prime}\), when \(\boldsymbol{A}\) is \(m\times n\). Furthermore, when \(\boldsymbol{A}\) is \(n\times n\) we have \[\begin{align*}
    \frac{\partial }{\partial \boldsymbol{x}}\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime }\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{x} &= (\boldsymbol{A}+\boldsymbol{A}^{\prime })\boldsymbol{x} \\
    &= 2\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{x},\text{\ if }\boldsymbol{A}=\boldsymbol{A}^{\prime }
\end{align*}\] When \(\boldsymbol{B}=\boldsymbol{B}^{\prime}\) is \(m\times m\) we obtain using the above rules that \[\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\partial }{\partial \boldsymbol{x}}\left( \boldsymbol{c}-\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{x}\right) ^{\prime }\boldsymbol{B}\left( \boldsymbol{c}-\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{x}\right)=-2\boldsymbol{A}^{\prime}\boldsymbol{B}\boldsymbol{c}+2\boldsymbol{A}^{\prime }\boldsymbol{B}\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{x}\ \ \ \text{(}\boldsymbol{c}\ m\times 1\text{\ and }\boldsymbol{A} m\times n\text{)}
\end{equation*}\]
Suppose now that \(\boldsymbol{A}\) (\(n\times n\)) is nonsymmetric and unrestricted (that is, the value \(a_{ij}\) is unrelated to the value \(a_{kl}\), when either \(i\neq k\) or \(j\neq l\)). Then \[\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\partial}{\partial \boldsymbol{A}}\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime }\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{x}\equiv \left[ \frac{\partial }{\partial a_{ij}}\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime }\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{x}\right] =[x_{i}x_{j}]=\boldsymbol{x}\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime }\ \ \text{(}\boldsymbol{x}\ n\times 1\text{\ and }\boldsymbol{A}\ n\times n\text{)}
\end{equation*}\] and \[\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\partial}{\partial \boldsymbol{A}}\log\det(\boldsymbol{A})=\left( \boldsymbol{A}^{-1}\right)^{\prime}=\left( \boldsymbol{A}^{\prime }\right)^{-1}
\end{equation*}\] The latter result uses the fact that \(\det (\boldsymbol{A})=\sum_{k}a_{ik}a_{ik}^{\ast }\), where \(a_{ik}^{\ast }\), the element of the adjoint matrix \(\boldsymbol{A}^{\ast}\) of \(\boldsymbol{A}\) (see Appendix (Matrixpoly), does not depend on \(a_{ij}\). By the chain rule we get \[\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\partial }{\partial a_{ij}}\log \det (\boldsymbol{A})=\frac{1}{\det (\boldsymbol{A})}\frac{\partial \det (\boldsymbol{A})}{\partial a_{ij}}=\frac{1}{\det (\boldsymbol{A})}a_{ij}^{\ast}=[\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}]_{ji}=\left[ \left( \boldsymbol{A}^{-1}\right) ^{\prime }\right]_{ij}
\end{equation*}\] where the third equation uses the formula of the inverse matrix \(\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}=\left(\det (\boldsymbol{A})\right) ^{-1}\boldsymbol{A}^{\ast}\) and the definition of the adjoint matrix. 
The last two results above do not hold when \(\boldsymbol{A}\) is symmetric. If \(\boldsymbol{A}(n\times n)\) is symmetric \(\boldsymbol{A}=\boldsymbol{A}^{\prime}\), then \(a_{ij}=a_{ji}\) and we obtain \[\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\partial}{\partial a_{ij}}\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{x}=\left\{
    \begin{array}{c}
        x_{i}^{2}\text{, when }i=j \\
        2x_{i}x_{j}\text{, when }i\neq j
    \end{array}
    \right.
\end{equation*}\] and \[\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\partial}{\partial a_{ij}}\log(\det(\boldsymbol{A}))=\left\{
    \begin{array}{c}
        \left[ \boldsymbol{A}^{-1}\right] _{ii}\text{, when }i=j \\
        2\left[ \boldsymbol{A}^{-1}\right] _{ji}\text{, when }i\neq j
    \end{array}
    \right.
\end{equation*}\] Using matrix notation these formulae can be expressed as \[\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\partial}{\partial \boldsymbol{A}}\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime }\boldsymbol{A}\boldsymbol{x}=2\boldsymbol{x}\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime }-\mathsf{diag}\left[\boldsymbol{x}\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime }\right]
\end{equation*}\] and \[\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\partial }{\partial \boldsymbol{A}}\log (\det (\boldsymbol{A}))=2\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}-\mathsf{diag}\left[\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}\right]
\end{equation*}\] where \(\mathsf{diag}\left[\boldsymbol{A}\right]\) indicates a diagonal matrix whose diagonal elements are those of \(\boldsymbol{A}\) (i.e., \(\mathsf{diag}\left[ \boldsymbol{A}\right]=[a_{ij}I(i=j)]_{ij}\), where \(I(i=j)=1\), if \(i=j\) and \(I(i=j)=0\), \(i\neq j\)).
Apply the above results on the log-likelihood function of the VAR model: \[\begin{equation*}
    l(\boldsymbol{\pi} ,\boldsymbol{\Sigma} )=\frac{T}{2}\log \left( \det (\boldsymbol{\Phi} )\right) -\frac{1}{2}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\boldsymbol{u} _{t}(\boldsymbol{\pi} )^{\prime }\boldsymbol{\Phi} \boldsymbol{u} _{t}(\boldsymbol{\pi} )
\end{equation*}\] We get \[\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\partial }{\partial \boldsymbol{\Phi} }\boldsymbol{u} _{t}(\boldsymbol{\pi} )^{\prime }\boldsymbol{\Phi}\boldsymbol{u}_{t}(\boldsymbol{\pi})=2\boldsymbol{u} _{t}(\boldsymbol{\pi} )\boldsymbol{u}_{t}(\boldsymbol{\pi})^{\prime}-\mathsf{diag}\left[\boldsymbol{u} _{t}(\boldsymbol{\pi} )\boldsymbol{u} _{t}(\boldsymbol{\pi} )^{\prime }\right]
\end{equation*}\] and (using the notation \(\boldsymbol{\Phi} ^{-1}=\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\)) \[\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\partial }{\partial \boldsymbol{\Phi} }\log \left( \det (\boldsymbol{\Phi} )\right) =2\boldsymbol{\Phi} ^{-1}-\mathsf{diag}\left[ \boldsymbol{\Phi} ^{-1}\right] =2\boldsymbol{\Sigma} -\mathsf{diag}\left[ \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \right]
\end{equation*}\] Now, the first order condition with respect to \(\boldsymbol{\Phi}\) of the likelihood function is seen to be \[\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\partial }{\partial \boldsymbol{\Phi} }l(\boldsymbol{\pi} ,\boldsymbol{\Sigma} ) = \frac{T}{2}\left\{ 2\boldsymbol{\Sigma} -\mathsf{diag}\left[ \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \right] \right\} -\frac{1}{2}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\left\{ 2\boldsymbol{u} _{t}(\boldsymbol{\pi} )\boldsymbol{u} _{t}(\boldsymbol{\pi})^{\prime}-\mathsf{diag}\left[ \boldsymbol{u} _{t}(\boldsymbol{\pi} )\boldsymbol{u} _{t}(\boldsymbol{\pi})^{\prime }\right] \right\}=\boldsymbol{0}
\end{equation*}\] By looking at this matrix equation element by element, one finds that it can be re-expressed as \[\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\partial }{\partial \boldsymbol{\Phi} }l(\boldsymbol{\pi} ,\boldsymbol{\Sigma} )=\frac{T}{2}\boldsymbol{\Sigma} -\frac{1}{2}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\boldsymbol{u} _{t}(\boldsymbol{\pi} )\boldsymbol{u} _{t}(\boldsymbol{\pi} )^{\prime }=\boldsymbol{0}
\end{equation*}\] We obtain the same result whether or not the differentiation operation takes the symmetry restriction into account.
